# Lead and Develop High-Performing Teams, Module 3 Lab

## Name the situation
- **Who they are (role, not name), what you have observed, and how long it has been happening.:** A product manager reporting to me has raised an executive-dashboard request in each of our last three weekly sprint-planning meetings, despite our agreed focus on field adoption. They believe the dashboard is necessary to protect two customer renewals. Reopening this discussion has reduced the time available to plan our field-experience improvements.

## Make a diagnosis
- **Your diagnosis, plus one sentence on why. Is your frustration with their behavior, or with a decision you made?:** Clarity: I established the priority but did not define how new commercial evidence could justify revisiting it. My frustration is with the repeated disruption, but it also reflects my decision to treat the dashboard as a hard no without establishing an exception process.

## Write your opening line
- **One next action I will take in the next two weeks is:** Meet privately with the product manager to understand the renewal evidence and acknowledge the missing escalation process. Within one week, I will review alternatives with the account owner and engineering lead, document the priority decision, and establish how new evidence should be raised before sprint planning. The following week, I will assess whether this keeps planning focused while allowing concerns to be heard.
- **The first sentence of the conversation I need to have is:** In our last three sprint-planning meetings, the dashboard request has reopened our agreed priorities and reduced planning time for field improvements, and I recognize that I haven’t made clear how we should revisit those priorities when renewals are at risk.

